package SystemDesign.LoggingFrameworkDemo;

public abstract class AbstractLogger {
    protected int level;
    protected AbstractLogger nextLoggingLevel;

    public void setNextLoggingLevel(
            AbstractLogger nextLoggingLevel) {
        this.nextLoggingLevel = nextLoggingLevel;
    }

    public void logMessage(int level, String msg) {
        if (this.level == level) {
            display(msg);
        }

        if (nextLoggingLevel != null) {
            nextLoggingLevel.logMessage(level, msg);
        }
    }

    protected abstract void display(String msg);
}



Explanation
- level: identifies the type of message handled by this logger.
- nextLoggingLevel: stores the next logger in the chain.
- setNextLoggingLevel(): links one logger to another.
- logMessage(): displays a message if the level matches, then passes it forward.
- display(): is abstract because each subclass defines its own output format.
Notice that your screenshot uses ==, not <=. This means each logger handles only its exact level.
