F. Logger.java
This is the main class exposed to the application. It implements the Singleton pattern.

package SystemDesign.LoggingFrameworkDemo;

import java.io.Serializable;

public class Logger implements Cloneable, Serializable {

    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

    private static volatile Logger logger;
    private transient AbstractLogger chainOfLogger;

    private Logger() {
        if (logger != null) {
            throw new IllegalStateException(
                "Object already created");
        }
    }

    public static Logger getInstance() {
        if (logger == null) {
            synchronized (Logger.class) {
                if (logger == null) {
                    logger = new Logger();
                    logger.chainOfLogger =
                        LogManager.buildChainOfLogger();
                }
            }
        }
        return logger;
    }

    @Override
    protected Object clone()
            throws CloneNotSupportedException {
        throw new CloneNotSupportedException();
    }

    protected Object readResolve() {
        return getInstance();
    }

    private void createLog(int level, String msg) {
        chainOfLogger.logMessage(level, msg);
    }

    public void info(String msg) {
        createLog(1, msg);
    }

    public void error(String msg) {
        createLog(2, msg);
    }

    public void debug(String msg) {
        createLog(3, msg);
    }
}



Explanation of the important methods
- getInstance() ensures that the application obtains a single shared Logger.
- synchronized (Logger.class) prevents multiple threads from creating separate instances simultaneously.
- The second if (logger == null) checks again after acquiring the lock. This is called double-checked locking.
- chainOfLogger holds the first node of the chain.
- createLog() passes the selected level and message to the chain.
- clone() prevents creating another instance through cloning.
- readResolve() ensures that deserialization returns the shared instance.
volatile helps ensure visibility of the initialized instance across threads. transient means the chain itself is not serialized; readResolve() obtains the singleton again after deserialization.
